I just tried compiling XFree86 4.2.x on Alpha with fairly bad=20
luck--more or less a segfault, see below:

--------
(WW) Open APM failed (/dev/apm_bios) (No such file or directory)
(II) Module ABI versions:
        XFree86 ANSI C Emulation: 0.1
        XFree86 Video Driver: 0.5
        XFree86 XInput driver : 0.3
        XFree86 Server Extension : 0.1
        XFree86 Font Renderer : 0.3
(II) Loader running on linux
(II) LoadModule: "bitmap"
(II) Loading /usr/X11R6/lib/modules/fonts/libbitmap.a
(II) Module bitmap: vendor=3D"The XFree86 Project"
        compiled for 4.2.0, module version =3D 1.0.0
        Module class: XFree86 Font Renderer
        ABI class: XFree86 Font Renderer, version 0.3

Fatal server error:
Caught signal 11.  Server aborting


When reporting a problem related to a server crash, please send
the full server output, not just the last messages.
This can be found in the log file "/var/log/XFree86.0.log".
Please report problems to xfree86@xfree86.org.
--------

The above is stock 4.2.0, with a patch applied from Thorsten=20
Kranzkowski for gcc-3.x compatibility in the XFree86 module=20
loader.  I've also tried 4.2.1 with similar results.  Since the=20
posting relating to this patch is apparently no longer in google=20
(it was yesterday...), I've attached a text copy of it below. =20
FYI, I'm using gcc-3.2.

Without the patch, XFree86 compiles on alpha but cannot load any=20
modules (thanks to the R_ALPHA_GPREL{HIGH,LOW} relocation types=20
being added somewhere around gcc-3.1).  With the patch, it seems=20
XFree86 crashes when trying to load the first module...

I have a few ideas on how to get around this, but I figured I'd=20
run them by some people on the list rather than spend another=20
3-4 hours building XFree86 for each wild goose-chase I go off=20
on:

1) A lot of this module loading trouble appears to spring from=20
XFree86 using a misbegotten module loader that monkeys around=20
with ELF format directly.  If I compile it to load modules the=20
proper, portable way (via the dlopen API), will it solve this=20
problem for good?  How well supported is this?

2) Do I need to manually specify compilation with -fPIC (via the=20
DefaultGcc2AxpOpts definition?)  It occurs to me that the=20
XFree86 build isn't smart enough to figure out how to=20
selectively apply this flag on its own.  Then again, considering=20
XFree86's jacked module loader, neither am I.

--=20
Kelledin
"If a server crashes in a server farm and no one pings it, does=20
it still cost four figures to fix?"
